    "story": [
      "On 14 July 1863 Abraham Lincoln drafted a letter to Major General George G. Meade. It was an unusual letter to leave unsent. The president wrote in anger about an opportunity that he believed had been lost after the Union victory at Gettysburg. The letter remained a draft. Meade did not receive this particular accusation, though the military problem that provoked it was real and urgent.",
      "A week earlier the Union and Confederate armies had collided near Gettysburg, Pennsylvania. Meade's Army of the Potomac had fought off Robert E. Lee's invasion of the North in a battle that cost tens of thousands of casualties across both sides. The result was an enormous Union success. Yet Lee's remaining army was able to retreat toward Virginia, and severe weather caused the Potomac River to rise. Lincoln believed that a decisive pursuit might prevent the Confederates from escaping.",
      "Lincoln was leading the United States through the Civil War, fought over the survival of the Union and, fundamentally, the institution of slavery. His decisions could affect the fate of armies and the political future of the country. He depended upon commanders who possessed military expertise and responsibility for soldiers in the field. A president could demand action, but he could not move an army by personal wish alone. The distinction between political urgency and operational risk was central to the disputes that developed around the pursuit.",
      "Meade had taken command shortly before Gettysburg. He was responsible for exhausted troops after a major battle and faced incomplete intelligence about enemy positions, terrain and river crossings. Those constraints did not make Lincoln's frustration imaginary. They remind us that the judgment being made in Washington was different from the one made near the army itself. The same battlefield could appear as a narrow chance for a decisive victory to the president and a dangerous operational problem to the general.",
      "The retreat gave Lincoln reason to fear that the war would continue at enormous human cost. In the draft he expressed disappointment that Lee's army had escaped. He was not commenting on a minor breach of etiquette. He believed a military advantage with potentially far-reaching consequences had been allowed to slip away. The words on paper were a direct way of assigning responsibility for that judgment.",
      "The draft is preserved among Lincoln's papers at the Library of Congress. Readers can inspect the manuscript, with the date, salutation and the president's critical remarks. The absence of delivery matters just as much as the content. A head of state had not simply forgotten to complain; he had formulated the complaint and then declined to make this document part of an exchange with the general.",
      "Why? Later writers have sometimes used this episode as a tidy moral illustration about controlling anger: write an angry letter, then put it away. That may be an attractive lesson, but it is not a substitute for reading the evidence. The manuscript documents the words and their non-delivery. It does not give us access to one final, fully recorded private calculation that can settle every question about Lincoln's choice.",
      "The effect of sending such a letter is nonetheless worth considering. Meade had just won a major battle and remained in command of a large army. A severe reproach from the president could shape a commander's confidence, reputation, and relationship with civilian leadership. Lincoln had to weigh not only whether his criticism was sincere, but also whether its delivery at that particular moment would help achieve the military objectives he wanted.",
      "The emotional reality of the draft is part of the archive. Lincoln's disappointment did not vanish because Meade never received the letter. Holding it back changed its social consequences rather than erasing its composition. It became a record of an impulse and an evaluation that the writer did not directly impose on the recipient.",
      "The story also offers a distinction between suppressing a truth and choosing a form of communication. Lincoln did continue to direct and assess the war; he did not permanently renounce his responsibility as commander in chief. The unsent letter concerns a specific rebuke and a specific addressee. The subject of our exhibition is not a president who stopped speaking, but a president who chose not to send one formulation of what he wanted to say.",
      "The document's survival creates a second audience. Meade did not have the chance to respond to it in 1863, but later historians and readers can study the words as evidence of Lincoln's thinking. The letter thus has two histories: an intervention that never reached its immediate target, and an archival record that became public long afterward.",
      "At the center remains a familiar human decision in unusually high-stakes circumstances. One can be convinced that a criticism is justified and still decide not to deliver it. The line between courage and restraint is difficult to locate from outside. Here we can inspect what Lincoln wrote; we must be more cautious about claiming to know every reason he withheld it."
    ],
